\details{
\strong{Workflow overview}

The scoring stage evaluates whether candidate patches generated
during deterministic segmentation show sufficient evidence to remain
within the high-confidence burned pool.

Conceptually, the workflow proceeds as follows:
\enumerate{
  \item evaluate internal geometric and contextual plausibility,
  \item assess external fire-support evidence when hotspot data are
    available,
  \item evaluate temporal consistency relative to previous-year
    burns,
  \item measure spectral consistency relative to high-confidence
    burned-like references,
  \item integrate all evidence into a final deterministic decision.
}

This stage remains fully deterministic and reproducible given
identical inputs and parameter settings.

\strong{Deterministic support references}

Spectral support in Filter 3 is always evaluated relative to a
keep-like reference distribution.

Two public reference modes are supported:

\strong{Same-year local fallback}

Use \code{keep_pool = NULL}.

The scoring engine automatically derives the support reference
internally from the early same-year keep core of the target year
being scored.

This is the native deterministic scoring mode and the default
workflow.

\strong{Explicit external reference}

Supply an explicit \code{keep_pool} built from trusted decision
layers belonging to other years.

This mode allows scoring against an externally controlled burned-like
reference distribution.

Explicit same-year \code{keep_pool} references are not a supported
public mode. They are methodologically circular and are rejected when
helper-generated metadata reveal that the explicit pool contains the
current \code{target_year}.

\strong{Details}

The deterministic scoring workflow is organised as a sequential
rule-based filtering system designed to distinguish:
\itemize{
  \item high-confidence burned patches as keep,
  \item ambiguous burned-like detections as review,
  \item likely false positives as drop,
}
while preserving full decision traceability.

\strong{Filter 1 -- Internal support and contextual plausibility}

Filter 1 evaluates:
\itemize{
  \item burned-seed support,
  \item burnable-context plausibility,
  \item vegetation availability,
  \item non-burnable contamination.
}

Candidate patches intersecting Stage-1 support remain on the keep
pathway.

Candidate patches without seed support are subsequently evaluated
relative to the burnable domain:
\itemize{
  \item burnable but unsupported patches transition to review,
  \item non-burnable patches transition to drop,
  \item candidates with excessive missing vegetation support may also
    be dropped.
}

Filter 1 is recorded through:
\itemize{
  \item `filter_1`
  \item `reason_1`
}

Filter 1 is independent from spectral-support references.

\strong{Filter 2 -- Hotspot corroboration}

Filter 2 evaluates external fire-support evidence using active-fire
hotspots when hotspot data are available for the target year.

In the canonical public implementation:
\itemize{
  \item hotspot-supported patches are mapped to `filter_2 = "keep"`,
  \item hotspot-free patches are mapped to `filter_2 = "review"`.
}

When hotspot data are unavailable, Filter 2 is recorded as not
applied.

Filter 2 is recorded through:
\itemize{
  \item `filter_2`
  \item `reason_2`
}

\strong{Temporal consistency assessment}

Temporal consistency is evaluated separately from Filters 1 to 3.

Overlap with previous-year burned areas is used to diagnose:
\itemize{
  \item residual old scars,
  \item repeated detections,
  \item temporal conflicts.
}

These diagnostics are recorded through:
\itemize{
  \item `preyear_overlap_frac`
  \item `preyear_action`
  \item `preyear_reason`
}

Temporal diagnostics are preserved as an auditable companion branch
within the deterministic workflow.

\strong{Filter 3 -- Spectral-support evaluation}

Filter 3 evaluates spectral consistency relative to the
keep-support distribution.

The workflow computes patch-level support metrics including:
\describe{
