This makes explicit references more conservative and externally
controlled than the native same-year fallback.

\strong{Final deterministic integration}

The outputs of Filters 1 to 3 are integrated into a final
deterministic confidence decision stored in \code{class_final}.

In broad terms:
\describe{
  \item{`keep`}{High-confidence burned patch.}
  \item{`review`}{Ambiguous or partially supported candidate.}
  \item{`drop`}{Likely false positive or contextually inconsistent
    detection.}
}

In the canonical public implementation, if exactly one active filter
drops a polygon while the remaining active filters keep it, the final
decision is softened to `review` rather than `drop`.

\strong{Why this stage matters}

Burned-area segmentation alone cannot reliably distinguish:
\itemize{
  \item true burned patches,
  \item agricultural disturbances,
  \item shadows,
  \item residual previous-year scars,
  \item fragmented low-support detections,
  \item spectrally ambiguous surfaces.
}

The deterministic scoring workflow provides:
\itemize{
  \item explicit decision traceability,
  \item reproducible filtering logic,
  \item interpretable confidence assignment,
  \item auditable burned-area reasoning.
}

Separating candidate generation from deterministic scoring improves
methodological transparency and facilitates downstream supervised
modelling and validation.

